[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89bbbb9c-5bf7-5e11-a192-421ca39a2b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 x^{2} - 6 + e^{- x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496575} {:answer/id #uuid "32a5418a-1e9b-4a09-b939-e21f730687f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^2+e^{-x}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89bbbb9c-5bf7-5e11-a192-421ca39a2b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 x^{2} - 6 + e^{- x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496575} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a9991b-4a5a-59bb-8999-d08fed4a1afe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "540280c5-2fb8-54b1-88f4-aa5261ba0b12" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $g'(x)=- 4 x- e^{- x}$ that satisfies the condition $g(0)=-5.$\n\n$g(x)=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ng'(x)=- 4 x- e^{- x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int g'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 4 x- e^{- x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ g(x) & =\\int (- 4 x)\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- e^{- x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-4)(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}) + (-1)(- e^{- x})+C \\\\ & =- 2 x^{2}+e^{- x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $g(x)=- 2 x^{2}+e^{- x}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $g(0)=-5$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =(- 2 (0)^{2}) + (e^{- (0)})+C \\\\ -5 & =(0) + (1)+C \\\\ C & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ng(x)=- 2 x^{2} - 6 + e^{- x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dd041ec-76f5-576c-b5f5-4ac1e33a875d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{15}{2} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496567} {:answer/id #uuid "71be9bd2-467c-544f-a86c-b94537352986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{17}{2} + \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496569} {:answer/id #uuid "7a56cab2-0b3b-50dc-8fac-bc8339e5496a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496572} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c9a11e-69a9-5c57-a016-135f4ee342f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- 4 x^{2} + 3 \\cos{(x )} - 12 + e^{- 2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496581} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e5a3f6-fd19-5031-bd08-3342f142ba83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{2 x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 \\sin{(x )}}{2} - \\frac{31}{4} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dd041ec-76f5-576c-b5f5-4ac1e33a875d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{15}{2} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496567} :answer-field/id #uuid "94031bbf-31fa-5e35-83b6-b4b9cede32c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7efbb8af-b76c-5365-a359-23b71e692cfb" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $f'(x)=- 4 x^{2}+e^{- 2 x}+3 \\cos{(x )}$ that satisfies the condition $f(0)=-8.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 4 x^{2}+e^{- 2 x}+3 \\cos{(x )}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 4 x^{2}+e^{- 2 x}+3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =\\int (- 4 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (e^{- 2 x})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-4)(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}) + (1)(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}) + (3)(\\sin{(x )})+C \\\\ & =- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}+3 \\sin{(x )}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}+3 \\sin{(x )}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0)=-8$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =(- \\frac{4 (0)^{3}}{3}) + (- \\frac{e^{- 2 (0)}}{2}) + (3 \\sin{((0) )})+C \\\\ -8 & =(0) + (- \\frac{1}{2}) + (0)+C \\\\ C & =- \\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nf(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{15}{2} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544429ba-196d-56ee-8bbe-1db7b23b9340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - 3 \\sin{(x )} - 6 + e^{- x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "544429ba-196d-56ee-8bbe-1db7b23b9340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - 3 \\sin{(x )} - 6 + e^{- x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496563} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b77f0f8-1f53-5123-abd8-4fb977688cb3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9b0a6a93-71df-5af7-be4e-1bb4244efcb6" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $g'(x)=- 4 x^{2}- e^{- x}- 3 \\cos{(x )}$ that satisfies the condition $g(0)=-5.$\n\n$g(x)=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ng'(x)=- 4 x^{2}- e^{- x}- 3 \\cos{(x )}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int g'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 4 x^{2}- e^{- x}- 3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ g(x) & =\\int (- 4 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- e^{- x})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- 3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-4)(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}) + (-1)(- e^{- x}) + (-3)(\\sin{(x )})+C \\\\ & =- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}+e^{- x}- 3 \\sin{(x )}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $g(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}+e^{- x}- 3 \\sin{(x )}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $g(0)=-5$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =(- \\frac{4 (0)^{3}}{3}) + (e^{- (0)}) + (- 3 \\sin{((0) )})+C \\\\ -5 & =(0) + (1) + (0)+C \\\\ C & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ng(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - 3 \\sin{(x )} - 6 + e^{- x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8f3b58-8858-5461-8c2f-f3fd0569f838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=- 2 x^{2} + \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496556} {:answer/id #uuid "57d4f7d9-ed6c-5a97-87e2-d6c53f06b31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=2 x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496564} {:answer/id #uuid "7694c46f-e60d-5d4f-a7ce-be776be3d5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=2 x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496570} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cac29f-14b5-5870-adf5-b3a40432c52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=4 x - e^{2 x} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496588} {:answer/id #uuid "f021c66e-4c17-5d8b-b6b0-3bdb846a1345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{4} - \\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57d4f7d9-ed6c-5a97-87e2-d6c53f06b31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=2 x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496564} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f9b219b-9380-5027-899f-fba5ea430d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afdd11d0-4e3c-5120-9f08-464acb94efd0" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $g'(x)=4 x- e^{2 x}$ that satisfies the condition $g(0)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ng'(x)=4 x- e^{2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int g'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4 x- e^{2 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ g(x) & =\\int (4 x)\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- e^{2 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(4)(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}) + (-1)(\\frac{e^{2 x}}{2})+C \\\\ & =2 x^{2}- \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $g(x)=2 x^{2}- \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $g(0)=-2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(2 (0)^{2}) + (- \\frac{e^{2 (0)}}{2})+C \\\\ -2 & =(0) + (- \\frac{1}{2})+C \\\\ C & =- \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ng(x)=2 x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46a51229-760d-59b7-9713-62d0167e9710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{(x )} - 5 + e^{- 2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46a51229-760d-59b7-9713-62d0167e9710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{(x )} - 5 + e^{- 2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496561} :answer-field/id #uuid "d54af8b6-64e9-50e8-a679-eed9f7d39cf4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff8b0291-a711-56fa-8ba2-9a74a6a7ab1c" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y'=- 6 x^{2}- 2 e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\cos{(x )}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=-4.$\n\n$y=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ny'=- 6 x^{2}- 2 e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\cos{(x )}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y'\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 6 x^{2}- 2 e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int (- 6 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- 2 e^{- 2 x})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- 2 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-6)(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}) + (-2)(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}) + (-2)(\\sin{(x )})+C \\\\ & =- 2 x^{3}+e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\sin{(x )}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=- 2 x^{3}+e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\sin{(x )}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(0)=-4$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =(- 2 (0)^{3}) + (e^{- 2 (0)}) + (- 2 \\sin{((0) )})+C \\\\ -4 & =(0) + (1) + (0)+C \\\\ C & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=- 2 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{(x )} - 5 + e^{- 2 x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f03a0e-89a7-5b16-bf01-1519355c07d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496747} {:answer/id #uuid "3a018c22-1a77-5e85-97e0-1ac9d6577bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496784} {:answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6f0f30-494e-5978-8c42-1f9efcb271d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497127} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5a577e-b9f2-5c3c-87c6-fb5029ee12ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf0e6db-e5f4-547e-8dad-d9a52bf566a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "284c9b33-1577-5c49-9cce-c96ae365ec69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76608" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x(1-{y}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bcde23-8fb4-54be-b4f9-12b3458b95b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496665} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8a9980-7371-5df1-8410-26fa8e6ff9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496683} {:answer/id #uuid "1989b09c-b976-5029-b925-43a8bcf85f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} {:answer/id #uuid "86a2fd99-6f60-54d4-ba6c-934c44c7e141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1989b09c-b976-5029-b925-43a8bcf85f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d46346-4c74-57e4-aaa3-613d6a4a40ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f217825-c491-59e7-9325-ea7e5e5d43ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-344285" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, where $f$ is $3$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $3$-periodic on $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, we have $L=\\frac{3}{2}$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496755} :answer-field/id #uuid "2216ef52-715e-5e56-a65f-58cd2ceb2ca9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67306212-e963-567c-a3f1-8493d029556a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166840" :question/problem "How many cakes can the pastry shop produce with $9$ packs of chocolate?\n\nThe pastry shop can produce$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes." :question/worked-solution "To produce $x$ cakes, the pastry shop uses $(\\frac{5x+3}{2})$ packs of chocolate. So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+3}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nWe need to solve this linear equation for $x.$\nFirst, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+3}{2} & =\\frac{9}{1} \\\\ (5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\ 5x+3 & =18 \\\\ 5x+3-3 & =18-3 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the pastry shop can produce $3$ cakes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3\\cos^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3\\cos^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d913041-76e7-5e6e-a8c0-9a8b344b6d06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74d3e4f5-83dd-544e-8625-a8bd48421738" :question/math-academy-id "q-352889" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{cos}^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({cos}^{2}⁡(t)-(-{cos}^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ec09a2-fbda-5a52-bb47-467878875739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496666} {:answer/id #uuid "44cab1fc-6315-55b0-b3f7-05e725aa34f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496806} {:answer/id #uuid "68265195-74f6-5911-84af-be7e3b4820a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496883} {:answer/id #uuid "c58ad0a2-710d-5e98-bc14-5b4a2dc8be59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497061} {:answer/id #uuid "d1293524-2d61-50b9-9952-84c3ff0dc264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1293524-2d61-50b9-9952-84c3ff0dc264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497094} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fdc3716-f94a-5460-8503-fc942472a7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b164922-7a86-5e35-904e-baea511723b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95012" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} & 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-7 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{7}{6}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{13}{2} & -13 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{13}{6} & \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{2}{x}_{2} &=-13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}{x}_{2} &=\\frac{13}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second, third and fourth equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5\\cdot 2 & =4 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =-6 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462d45d4-2587-5767-bbda-82dc90eb9b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496809} {:answer/id #uuid "480be999-cd10-5a4f-83f5-02eb7f7b0275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496819} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1a635-ce6b-5e3d-a212-6ca66dbb9f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0f4e40-8515-583c-aa83-8de5f9788688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1a635-ce6b-5e3d-a212-6ca66dbb9f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a94d15-fe13-5d85-9f7d-69d7a8a6ed69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed7b5966-6097-5c94-afc7-cde3e72271b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344323" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5063f8a0-899b-5e4f-b70e-5fbeab173e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496834} {:answer/id #uuid "cf933d00-1cd0-58a3-b054-9a9b5f189803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497086} {:answer/id #uuid "df8562cf-2270-52ce-9299-2a3db48074d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497137} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e14dc5-18ed-5023-8fe2-0751bffc21d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497158} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3568be-b178-5f3b-b7ec-36b1e63a80e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df8562cf-2270-52ce-9299-2a3db48074d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497137} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6ba6fef-e5a6-577a-86ab-a507607e574c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8afba66-d1aa-558e-99f9-1c7592f06bb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131232" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,2\\sqrt{2}t,\\,4⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}⟨4t,\\,\\sqrt{2}(4-{t}^{2}),\\,-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(4t)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}(4-{t}^{2}))}^{2}+(-4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+2{(4-{t}^{2})}^{2}+16{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+(32-16{t}^{2}+2{t}^{4})+16{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{t}^{4}+16{t}^{2}+32} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2({t}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8{t}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{{t}^{2}+4})}{{t}^{2}+4}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e6265d-627c-5bfe-b4a4-c9064cb498a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497959} {:answer/id #uuid "460eaa64-2244-54be-b725-8a2f2bf21ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497960} {:answer/id #uuid "576b3890-6abd-55de-92db-73d43f720e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498006} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd59b4d-9677-5eda-a75f-2e81639dd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498097} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf3078c-b7b8-5e5f-a2a5-c5b18a6edb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "576b3890-6abd-55de-92db-73d43f720e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498006} :answer-field/id #uuid "6accb65d-487d-5e4c-9f58-e9f7512e092a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "157c2ce3-5327-5267-a129-7f9aebf9b49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115560" :question/problem "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=3$ when $x=\\frac{5}{3},$ find the value of $y$ when $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=3$ when $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =\\frac{k}{(\\frac{5}{3})} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} & =k \\\\ \\frac{15}{3} & =k \\\\ 5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=2$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e41d7eb-d6b5-5b2c-946b-12009ab1f71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498095} {:answer/id #uuid "9784723c-c70e-51dd-9e53-5d403867e4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498143} {:answer/id #uuid "b24df18f-c0d7-55b0-a620-52ca08dd9606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498198} {:answer/id #uuid "b803ed2a-92df-50aa-86b4-a74a3d347a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498209} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e978c6-725e-52b7-a7d5-e19dd3beea3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b24df18f-c0d7-55b0-a620-52ca08dd9606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498198} :answer-field/id #uuid "7738aed2-6969-583c-b1d2-159e3752029b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19618a93-f5c7-57b8-b5e4-4ba96b1ca9c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-51816" :question/problem "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=10$ when $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ find the value of $y$ when $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=10$ when $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{k}{(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ 10\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}) & =k \\\\ \\frac{30}{2} & =k \\\\ 15 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{15}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=3$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{15}{3}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56e78bfe-9b51-5648-bc1f-b326bed0ae1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498005} {:answer/id #uuid "978e1395-42f6-59da-be0b-d636897c86b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4844" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498145} {:answer/id #uuid "b232cea5-5b98-5d1e-aef9-7f111da7c9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4314" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498197} {:answer/id #uuid "dac1216a-a2d9-5440-8a5e-99b9a41b6363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498286} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6d116c-6828-5758-80e7-52fc119e9bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4246" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dac1216a-a2d9-5440-8a5e-99b9a41b6363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498286} :answer-field/id #uuid "effd6099-f4df-5d9b-a019-343ae34a858c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bd1cff1-6c69-5338-8c54-dfce19778dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-152011" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(1,{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 18,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(16\\le Y\\le 20),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18} & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =N(18,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(16\\le Y\\le 20).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(16\\le Y\\le 20) & =P(\\frac{16-18}{\\sqrt{9}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{20-18}{\\sqrt{9}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{3}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.67\\le Z\\le 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2P(Z\\ge 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2(1-P(Z\\le 0.67)) \\\\  & =2P(Z\\le 0.67))-1 \\\\  & =2Φ(0.67)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.67)=0.7486.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(16\\le Y\\le 20)=2Φ(0.67)-1=0.4972.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0651e04-cc61-5bcf-af3b-f91d6f9a0b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{4},1)$, radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498158} {:answer/id #uuid "ac362cb6-492d-5473-b6dc-d32427af7ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$, radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498179} {:answer/id #uuid "db06cb6f-0562-5273-a873-d0e8e28fdf46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498288} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb9f323-6fcc-523f-9e46-a8019383f7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{4},-1)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498294} {:answer/id #uuid "f048c8b8-0deb-58cc-8917-7eb188230fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$, radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac362cb6-492d-5473-b6dc-d32427af7ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$, radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498179} :answer-field/id #uuid "d763b4a3-5d4a-5059-b073-6cac4252989e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d79c8724-85a2-50fb-b60d-a80008511394" :question/math-academy-id "q-103893" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x-2y=\\frac{11}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x-2y & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and its radius is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "617286e8-47ce-51b6-b506-3b48cff8cb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498029} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd99149-aedb-5119-a535-5559ad7a90f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498093} {:answer/id #uuid "884b8e65-9503-5538-83b3-f52c4b72fe28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{d}a+\\text{d}b+2\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498114} {:answer/id #uuid "89575744-0951-5e71-9a75-60b9e81bb12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{d}a+4\\,\\text{d}b+\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498116} {:answer/id #uuid "cab3f89e-5562-5702-943e-6433f814978e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{d}a+\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab3f89e-5562-5702-943e-6433f814978e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{d}a+\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498250} :answer-field/id #uuid "361b4f31-81d9-5f43-b63b-1aa713ee6c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1b5df81-7853-5e90-b2a0-96b74729c9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79749" :question/problem "Given a right triangular prism, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ if the height is $h=1$ and the base is a right triangle with legs $a=2$ and $b=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a given right triangular prism is $V=\\frac{abh}{2}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂a})\\,\\text{d}a+(\\frac{∂V}{∂b})\\,\\text{d}b+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{bh}{2}\\,\\text{d}a+\\frac{ah}{2}\\,\\text{d}b+\\frac{ab}{2}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=2,$ $b=4,$ and $h=1,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(2,4,1) & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{2}\\,\\text{d}a+\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{2}\\,\\text{d}b+\\frac{2\\cdot 4}{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{d}a+\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c97c988-3336-5cff-9def-ec7413102202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(u+w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498757} {:answer/id #uuid "1c002a85-7881-5d55-b3a3-c0858f2a52d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(u+v-w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498789} {:answer/id #uuid "449afe6a-6893-523f-8969-4d9facf23e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(v-w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498886} {:answer/id #uuid "5760564c-6d75-5aa4-a9ab-7873348de3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u-w-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498930} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc9fd42-c83a-5ec5-bb24-9d73b0ba363e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(v-w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c97c988-3336-5cff-9def-ec7413102202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(u+w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498757} :answer-field/id #uuid "219a7147-3bde-5244-974f-5c2639b194f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21557ab9-79b3-55a0-a104-51c3d273599e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161868" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le \\frac{2x-y}{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\frac{y}{2}\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=6.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |6|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,6(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d23c71b1-938d-5c3c-896d-64a1b9e36fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d23c71b1-938d-5c3c-896d-64a1b9e36fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499197} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ead61a9-5da2-511e-a657-d00ea95b48b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d1e7c48-2675-551e-94d8-a9622a621313" :question/math-academy-id "q-345097" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{42}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{144}{25})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1764+20736}{625}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{22500}{625}}=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34df0886-f589-5a77-91fd-b574dff15d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498850} {:answer/id #uuid "4869d8e8-8306-5d02-89de-d7b839a472df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498896} {:answer/id #uuid "669efc65-a593-55a2-9cb2-e9832fd6adb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498959} {:answer/id #uuid "9d702685-480a-51e1-930a-6e01b50056fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499064} {:answer/id #uuid "b0037796-de78-55f7-b178-e17d9506d50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4869d8e8-8306-5d02-89de-d7b839a472df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498896} :answer-field/id #uuid "efd8c6c1-d268-5074-8c20-054b800150f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ee83331-c90f-5e77-b2f4-e2b981936cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-82157" :question/problem "Find $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{(-\\frac{a}{2x})\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{2x})\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{2x})} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(-\\frac{a}{2x})}^{3}} \\\\ &= -\\frac{a}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17418eda-e303-583e-abb8-8163f34cf65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498779} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f2bf6-1e84-5da8-8223-f7a8617abe63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa17f5a-3932-5a85-be4a-9e7f8aca50c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499103} {:answer/id #uuid "af9d5967-0e62-5e9c-80be-bb9563606efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499116} {:answer/id #uuid "e507ffc4-50dc-56ba-8db0-2ce573a2305a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f2bf6-1e84-5da8-8223-f7a8617abe63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} :answer-field/id #uuid "06575685-8b6e-5313-b708-d919a2eb705b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e32ec93-9a95-5b63-b1e8-8733a4d5c7af" :question/math-academy-id "q-82910" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curves $(x+y)^{3}-y=0$ and $(x+y)^{2}-y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{3}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{3}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{3} \\\\ v &={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{3}\\le v\\le {u}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8d2549c665a918cc8d69c69136fcb49e654ebb9aa5e53611155798e791b36a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{u}^{3})du \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39bd8942-8ea6-5f03-b9c2-980c08387095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}+{e}^{-3\\pi /2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498858} {:answer/id #uuid "3f95ea7f-6aac-57fb-89fc-e3618b1effd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{3}{e}^{3\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498876} {:answer/id #uuid "472465ce-5697-565a-b045-a23526f9815c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}({e}^{2\\pi /3}-{e}^{-2\\pi /3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498893} {:answer/id #uuid "a7929dc7-bc71-5a90-8bb2-c5a85e81928b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499099} {:answer/id #uuid "e97e245e-6898-5e7c-8a99-3baaa90c0589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{3\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7929dc7-bc71-5a90-8bb2-c5a85e81928b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499099} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f08aa4-9c3b-5881-9036-fcd624473552" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "657b78ff-bfd8-5861-b182-c1815c474783" :question/math-academy-id "q-9534" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=2{e}^{3\\theta }$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2{e}^{3\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=6{e}^{3\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}+{(6{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{6\\theta }+36{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{40{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}{e}^{3\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e7704d1-16de-5f57-9381-77fa91a75339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498946} {:answer/id #uuid "65e56828-40a4-5c88-88b8-dc7bcfb344e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-t}+\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498957} {:answer/id #uuid "74d13041-41ad-5e74-9ece-8544d7624726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498988} {:answer/id #uuid "ae716747-adb4-5e5e-89d4-b2a39710996d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499114} {:answer/id #uuid "b83a0cfa-c6af-5ba3-83d9-9e7b4d506e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e7704d1-16de-5f57-9381-77fa91a75339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498946} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebb96b46-f989-5842-a663-f156f4929adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "676e6c66-ab4f-5266-9314-934677a34712" :question/math-academy-id "q-345156" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{21}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-5t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{21}{2}-6I-5q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{21}{2}-6I(0)-5q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-6\\cdot 1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{12}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-(0)}+B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-5B{e}^{-5t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-A{e}^{-(0)}-5B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 2 & =-A-5B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=A+B \\\\ 2 &=-A-5B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $B=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9b4ef9-8eb6-5c5c-b433-5ffc4c7236ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498824} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8c6c00-5b20-53bc-acb7-b0b31ab2298d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498947} {:answer/id #uuid "88676aa6-a08b-583a-a6f9-2618bd726361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{12}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499034} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e226dc-7e0d-534a-9b5e-f226b2636d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{12}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499239} {:answer/id #uuid "f378af41-a686-5292-9362-123eea7e70ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1e226dc-7e0d-534a-9b5e-f226b2636d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{12}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499239} :answer-field/id #uuid "fecc3609-91c7-5f66-a205-ec1648ad9bd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77ed6de7-60cd-57e5-95ef-8b1b61a0f9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-160638" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{{e}^{x}},\\,y=\\frac{12}{{e}^{x}},\\,2y-1=0,\\,2y-5=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}y=5,\\,{e}^{x}y=12,\\,2y=1,\\,2y=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x}y,\\,v=2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={e}^{x}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}y=5\\,\\text{ }\\,⟹\\,u=5 \\\\  & {e}^{x}y=12\\,⟹\\,u=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 2y=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,5\\le u\\le 12,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/61/615d6822e60e9b5ff646497e14df1468e1fd09ee67fab5c334b635d130f16f14.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x}y & {e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}y \\\\  & =2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{12}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{12}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1784c964-2bd8-551a-9e62-6cb6f2560ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498780} {:answer/id #uuid "255b1548-0862-5d3a-8e4b-d22ea5aa0f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498812} {:answer/id #uuid "4f74f069-41cb-54fc-8a9a-08d22d7c1865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498916} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9780c4-afc1-59e7-ac65-84b80ad23ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499011} {:answer/id #uuid "903bd708-b320-50fc-b991-e52faae4df28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d9780c4-afc1-59e7-ac65-84b80ad23ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499011} :answer-field/id #uuid "2030cf44-2880-55da-b8da-7f5dffbc7894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "787730fc-5924-57cd-ac3a-f6e87e085583" :question/math-academy-id "q-161893" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n∭(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot {[{e}^{v}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (e-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8fe484-3889-58d1-8f1f-caa851dbee11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498835} {:answer/id #uuid "40230e03-b9ac-5a57-8432-36ead7d33681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498877} {:answer/id #uuid "686a3a2f-5456-57f4-bf33-92993d1268ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498966} {:answer/id #uuid "b10740ac-3d00-5d70-a025-0645fac307c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499123} {:answer/id #uuid "e06340a0-7e4c-5772-a063-3ea903fbcb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "686a3a2f-5456-57f4-bf33-92993d1268ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498966} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b67e92f-d71e-5f0f-ba51-6ad73ad30d84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fcf62b8-f859-5393-a4f9-53904a00d462" :question/math-academy-id "q-345133" :question/problem "![](images/12/12a73555c78b324406714337b999b12a2166559eb1795236854e29747e88a5b1.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{P}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (4\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-6\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},$ $\\beta =\\frac{2}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-3,$ $Q=2,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-3+2=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308ed31c-23c6-5076-9326-a4c8719125ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498842} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb97491-dba6-5589-9683-9f48dd09761c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{2}q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498948} {:answer/id #uuid "a1aa3515-ef91-55c3-b692-ae8e2eb1e1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499075} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb0366a-9b57-5e7e-bef8-5e55731b9195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499178} {:answer/id #uuid "d1098f1c-859a-50c4-99fe-1eb8a2a4e374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "308ed31c-23c6-5076-9326-a4c8719125ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498842} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c02197-355c-50c6-989b-28bd28b9ed42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80b3a60d-0575-5aee-af41-4b72c4744aed" :question/math-academy-id "q-345106" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/58/58cde9ddaa098a14ebb26bb70128f018f0c5892214815cc2d3057c9881a9546f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=8\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+16q+8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0989efb5-1038-59ac-8680-60117382e68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498746} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce9d6eb-3f8f-56eb-a2d0-a4cfacaf7268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498792} {:answer/id #uuid "38d06719-2aef-52ec-b523-72bbe2d5ce07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498855} {:answer/id #uuid "51cc30b4-2098-5551-9641-1f9551e409a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498920} {:answer/id #uuid "52b44f14-c340-50a7-9cae-50d08c357aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51cc30b4-2098-5551-9641-1f9551e409a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498920} :answer-field/id #uuid "f107053f-9248-55df-a0bd-cf3499751023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85d77d15-6969-5117-952d-e2b202d58514" :question/math-academy-id "q-15123" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{({\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12666955-acda-50b1-a759-415719031788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498766} {:answer/id #uuid "33260567-19af-5f87-8391-f7b7b20532c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498847} {:answer/id #uuid "a6520622-46f1-5688-af38-61a3ff107da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499095} {:answer/id #uuid "c17ed11d-1615-505b-9ee3-f62b086faf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499157} {:answer/id #uuid "faf10f5c-fe15-5f32-843c-38243f61e787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6520622-46f1-5688-af38-61a3ff107da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499095} :answer-field/id #uuid "772a5b09-0846-503c-9495-bc958a42c8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92990ecc-5ba0-56e3-944d-6e84f299eb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-162210" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-{e}^{x},\\,v=y,\\,w=z{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & 0 & {e}^{-x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & {e}^{-x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4a70e6b-af1c-5577-8af3-126ce8b4aabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4a70e6b-af1c-5577-8af3-126ce8b4aabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499246} :answer-field/id #uuid "6345212c-d6d1-5b93-bb17-d2f3f24d44d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a920ee2-be49-53c0-8384-6f4e796b9132" :question/math-academy-id "q-345126" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $32\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{16}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/23/23e8796d3977632278d4d393510a30df974634750a1c02f56fd8bacbe3b5d57a.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=32\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=32I=32\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/16)}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=8\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+32\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+16q=12.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n8(0)+32(0)+16q=12,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n16q=12\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{12}{16}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{4}$ coulombs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25cdb946-258e-53a2-b8f8-2ab57e235521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498813} {:answer/id #uuid "5740c8e7-55d8-5593-be17-37b3da74f6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498929} {:answer/id #uuid "7432af23-f90c-521c-82d6-f4de37bca049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.1q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498985} {:answer/id #uuid "e39afebc-fd32-5996-9855-d2bef0af1a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499242} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fc04d2-ee9b-5d88-9e30-1df86999a8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.1q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e39afebc-fd32-5996-9855-d2bef0af1a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499242} :answer-field/id #uuid "762f659e-79b7-5a6e-95e6-304faf42e2f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9cd80162-3ef8-51ba-9ca2-79d0022c1e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-345111" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f6c545a697bfa58794995f802950e509e3df63bd3d34db957ee3556461ec03f.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $16\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.025\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.025}=40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+40q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040577a1-f488-586f-a244-5ac52452a051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498738} {:answer/id #uuid "134f7fbf-b645-5f5e-a680-2cf1d5fc853c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498772} {:answer/id #uuid "7649b34b-2218-533c-b3c5-146847fddc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498997} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d46a27-dd36-5701-8dd1-b3331e4e3270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499086} {:answer/id #uuid "e83877aa-f978-5e05-a20d-0069c96a2685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4d46a27-dd36-5701-8dd1-b3331e4e3270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499086} :answer-field/id #uuid "612abc3f-006a-54a9-ba2c-ed70e2955dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad40770e-3352-5877-ade7-ccfd965beccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162185" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-y,\\,v=x,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f76ca2e-6813-528d-b05a-1cbae894cd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498798} {:answer/id #uuid "31287e2e-19cf-5f18-8334-37c5ad1ac19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498843} {:answer/id #uuid "38d08ad7-d775-5da0-9791-be3349a2c3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498856} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddc27d2-9734-50a3-b245-b012f402c28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498872} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cecd57-7009-5476-b23e-18da5400c0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ddc27d2-9734-50a3-b245-b012f402c28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498872} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f9234df-4595-59b2-aa42-20eb5e74670b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cb8dd05b-77ee-5cc1-8581-cca32ddb619d" :question/math-academy-id "q-345145" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e03b65f8301526be5ff2ce5ec3c7f8b896906bfef2a6308d2125e93f0ecf631.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $90\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=90\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=90I=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/200)}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+20q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+20{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+9(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +20(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (16\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-18\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha +18\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha +16\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{18}{145},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{36}{145})}^{2}+{(\\frac{32}{145})}^{2}}≈0.33,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160e2a0c-f074-5874-a5f2-17cc1fc2cae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498777} {:answer/id #uuid "6d20f288-51ab-5e95-ad79-fa5df49f89a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498973} {:answer/id #uuid "a35283a7-4adc-5de1-826d-87a387cd4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499081} {:answer/id #uuid "c87b6998-b8e9-5934-9893-be14c9c09927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499169} {:answer/id #uuid "d102d768-01d6-510f-bd39-ea2452eac6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a35283a7-4adc-5de1-826d-87a387cd4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499081} :answer-field/id #uuid "cafcbac5-e575-5e1d-93e5-5e527facd2e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d774fdc1-484c-5e5e-b140-afbc35a348d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-51202" :question/problem "![](images/87/87a3563153fe0310767a79a96df0df5f6c0824027776b761df4a792b8b9b235e.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "465a9a31-c9a7-5242-b06e-83d1de732242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{10}}{32{v}^{20}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498892} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea53a6a-43cc-5424-b0c0-f4959baa4754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{7}}{32{v}^{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498913} {:answer/id #uuid "6842d70f-c788-545a-96c5-2eb791e9c865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{u}^{5}}{16{v}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498965} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cdef2e-ff7f-5848-8193-597d13abef49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{u}^{6}}{8{v}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499100} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5a67c3-eb72-52e8-8d6e-906807a56f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{10}}{2{v}^{20}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "465a9a31-c9a7-5242-b06e-83d1de732242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{10}}{32{v}^{20}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498892} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4acd8be-661d-5d96-83bb-5a718612e8be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0110074-4c62-517f-b5cb-ff4cff4e0579" :question/math-academy-id "q-22838" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{-2{v}^{4}})}^{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{-2{v}^{4}})}^{5} &= \\frac{{({u}^{2})}^{5}}{{(-2{v}^{4})}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule in the numerator and the power of product rule in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{({u}^{2})}^{5}}{{(-2{v}^{4})}^{5}} &= \\frac{{u}^{2\\cdot 5}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {({v}^{4})}^{5}} \\\\ &= \\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {({v}^{4})}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {({v}^{4})}^{5}} &= \\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {v}^{4\\cdot 5}} \\\\ &= \\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-32)\\cdot {v}^{20}} \\\\ &= -\\frac{{u}^{10}}{32{v}^{20}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fdfeeca-c3d7-5d09-9a71-f864d8a8b7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498800} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6b0d94-eb76-52a5-b259-da50f984cfb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498943} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a46c95-6fde-5fa7-8bc5-e88f21d169ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499120} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ff2020-1de0-5813-9759-f90d2787076d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499166} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd8c843-c34b-5a57-b6ff-ef052ba33dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6ff2020-1de0-5813-9759-f90d2787076d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499166} :answer-field/id #uuid "332d65df-34cc-548f-ad19-ac000012253d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2a6b6c4-abd9-5fd2-be45-a09229653766" :question/math-academy-id "q-345151" :question/problem "![](images/72/725398d9e534fbc888ef9963c9075538aa70cde9317c664b253394fb63cff052.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+5(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +\\,4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +15\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-15\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +15\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -15\\alpha -5\\beta &=\\frac{16}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{24}{125},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{72}{125})}^{2}+{(\\frac{24}{125})}^{2}}≈0.61,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e8b8abf-8932-5377-9152-a6b4049fa212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498874} {:answer/id #uuid "b25a4fe9-8db6-57d0-b8ed-faa4b47a685e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499128} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b404c5-802e-5a1a-a90d-3dcb65998bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499238} {:answer/id #uuid "f97b8d8b-bc73-57de-b5fa-e8f9bbe0efe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499290} {:answer/id #uuid "fb95efed-9536-58af-96fd-52fa8b8b750c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e8b8abf-8932-5377-9152-a6b4049fa212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498874} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ecb4ea2-b9c4-5db0-9f41-8f3cda9910e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f652ccf1-f487-574b-9e94-0f12f4efc494" :question/math-academy-id "q-345098" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $60\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/e1/e13f3dd8d881385444b215f62e0c43d6eb40d36e4b6af5f4792cba0502eb2ea2.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=20\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500098} :answer-field/id #uuid "a07a90e1-3f2d-527c-95e4-9e7865dfa008" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f2920eb-bbfa-5091-822d-b1cc3cc2b1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344607" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate is the diagonal of the square decreasing when the diagonal is $20\\text{ cm}$ long?\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-10.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $20\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $h=20.$ Substituting this information into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal is decreasing at $0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f9369b-e6c7-5f61-8fc5-c873059be79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499814} {:answer/id #uuid "762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499872} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec08695-a478-5d1f-a7ad-7cc9d9bedfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499933} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf8fb62-6cae-52c3-b37d-05d7c31d9523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500076} {:answer/id #uuid "df357ea5-8c84-50a9-8a37-c8ef860e991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499872} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e91639-868e-5146-8866-35d968fb2a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "850324c7-bb98-55b1-99ad-faf0afddb108" :question/math-academy-id "q-2777" :question/problem "![](images/19/199f1c7e18eb0c25bf7caafb8e2e71fa3e0165af81083ea57c1fc16a74935ab3.png)\n\nA square's diagonals decrease at the rate of  $2\\text{ m}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square decreasing when the diagonals are $3\\text{ m}$ long each?" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{m/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=3.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 3=-6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499693} {:answer/id #uuid "a772f7bf-13d7-5a11-b09f-eb30c2bf7b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499978} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8e0339-f5c6-51f4-989a-0cdb574502ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499994} {:answer/id #uuid "c44d2824-b8b1-5867-82fe-eb017c5a781d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500051} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3e88a9-534e-5c2c-95bc-3fa313944feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499693} :answer-field/id #uuid "a33fd242-f46e-5e85-8083-9fc0a177d7df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fede564-db3c-5749-8f57-8bfacce22a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-48375" :question/problem "![](images/97/97a7d465f8412c88367ed3cf148af3ce387441284c10e3f84888c0186fa597ce.png)\n\nPeter wants to cut a piece of square-shaped glass while maintaining its shape. The area of this glass is decreasing at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate are the diagonals of the glass decreasing if the length of these diagonals is $50\\text{ in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=50.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot (-1.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1.5}{50} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the glass are decreasing at a rate of $0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f0116c-d24c-501d-a2e3-6d64ae73f071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499660} {:answer/id #uuid "4f094944-ced0-5da6-beff-36966c6a9bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499804} {:answer/id #uuid "5899b9f1-4d49-51f2-8d77-aac18ae3041c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499825} {:answer/id #uuid "84d56836-dc1c-5c3e-88b2-8e486dbb45fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499910} {:answer/id #uuid "aacd61b0-026b-5712-a0a0-1b3c6315b32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84d56836-dc1c-5c3e-88b2-8e486dbb45fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499910} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffacf0a3-cce6-5d50-befc-250fcc891555" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94724c66-b6e2-52f7-8122-f7efcf9307aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-111515" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$, and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${v}_{4}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${v}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{4}}=\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 3=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500052} {:answer/id #uuid "11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520802} :answer-field/id #uuid "540ea305-c7f1-5022-950d-9653c1a23171" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2135fd1-33ca-5d7e-9987-278b24125900" :question/math-academy-id "q-344664" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef685549b562c570914bc8b5b5066cb621e42c8536586b37467afc6c1eb6c194.png)\n\nAn adjustable square skylight is being expanded as part of a building renovation. The area of the skylight is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals increasing when each diagonal measures $18\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe diagonal is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square skylight, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=27.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=18.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 27 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =1.5\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the skylight is increasing at a rate of \n$1.5\\text{ mm/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d5f79a-7cdd-5989-a575-2e2a2e47857b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d5f79a-7cdd-5989-a575-2e2a2e47857b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499777} :answer-field/id #uuid "d025d04b-35c6-550c-86ed-3b8dbfd3c675" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6918689-3a92-5cad-ac6b-0de2903bcad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-166811" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction, the exact area of ​​each wall is$\\,${{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The exact area of  ​each wall is ${(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{17}{4}\\times \\frac{17}{4}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\times 17}{4\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}=\\frac{289}{16}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3811dc15-f044-5933-86e0-b81332887411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500581} {:answer/id #uuid "575b1543-5913-5111-9e9c-4cf207869497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500645} {:answer/id #uuid "5e277c6c-dcd2-5491-b1f3-c7fa6def4dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500660} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c8e6c-8875-5f05-83c5-ba640b983ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500687} {:answer/id #uuid "be36abcb-60b4-5b59-8d60-819c63d7b780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be36abcb-60b4-5b59-8d60-819c63d7b780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500862} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4374709-dd94-564d-a093-c34e0f6d470b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00e54e6a-cd0f-51b4-97b3-2489c07dd2c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97574" :question/problem "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61d7cc13-045d-5dfc-8f21-557e9035974d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60t^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61d7cc13-045d-5dfc-8f21-557e9035974d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60t^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500669} :answer-field/id #uuid "9181ccb7-fad9-5675-90e4-730835fd6807" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501083} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e9395c-263f-51bd-b6a4-d472df228bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{t^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51e9395c-263f-51bd-b6a4-d472df228bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{t^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500636} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2548aa-0d72-568a-936f-6eb2f6da4f5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3300ef54-fdde-5f40-809c-0c4bab2d0166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e^{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3300ef54-fdde-5f40-809c-0c4bab2d0166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e^{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500576} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0de4306-091e-529b-ae8c-530f65ea903f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cc15b80-72fe-59a5-9238-583abab2565e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350802" :question/problem "Determine ${f}^{″}(t)$ given that $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\sqrt{t} \\\\ {e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -2{t}^{6}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{″}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{1-2t})\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{6})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ -2{e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -12{t}^{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{e}^{1-2t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-12{t}^{5})\\,\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3/2}} \\\\ 4{e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -60{t}^{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c7b9ffa-61e8-5834-acc7-13454c3bc5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c7b9ffa-61e8-5834-acc7-13454c3bc5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500619} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc60e61-7c99-51fb-8ae6-518b9c8d435c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18e9c87c-d5d7-52d0-922d-e65af5fe041b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171258" :question/problem "Express ${(\\frac{2}{9})}^{-1}$ as an improper fraction in its simplest form.\n\n${(\\frac{2}{9})}^{-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expression ${(\\frac{2}{9})}^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $\\frac{2}{9}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{9})}^{-1}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20969546-8cf5-58bf-9f69-aa85c2b994ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500533} {:answer/id #uuid "4346ba55-161e-55f0-af3a-5ede323b90c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{83}{4}+{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500596} {:answer/id #uuid "a7969ead-7a86-53c0-8879-105a6fba4435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500813} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ca5c4a-5384-5160-a988-e4dc1ad1f8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500944} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5f00d8-7485-5f2b-815c-34d67d987271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5f00d8-7485-5f2b-815c-34d67d987271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501011} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a58e43-e215-570e-85ca-38eb345f6b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "464780e0-2b69-5233-beb8-cdc5f928ae62" :question/math-academy-id "q-142072" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-\\frac{1}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4} & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cc5889c-f956-5805-828b-a07b04225e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500725} {:answer/id #uuid "805a1efe-9356-5170-a269-8ed6c903aa17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500733} {:answer/id #uuid "d93ffed7-ac55-5ecb-a0d9-3189b1b4cd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500929} {:answer/id #uuid "d96310d9-2183-516b-b152-b846ac135e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500930} {:answer/id #uuid "f499ebac-94ab-572a-a984-e07f21d31ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96310d9-2183-516b-b152-b846ac135e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500930} :answer-field/id #uuid "28822793-1aa6-5555-91d5-42b23da22347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "517afe53-217c-5ede-9811-b9bf164398f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140380" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{y}^{2}+2y+\\frac{4}{5}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5$. So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{4}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}) & =-5({(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05373043-ede4-5f3b-ab00-6c5eb28bd928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500476} {:answer/id #uuid "43f6eebb-f90b-5e3e-bf7f-5a7b4d0e7ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500601} {:answer/id #uuid "510f8649-056c-567e-b3ee-8b8aa7b327bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500633} {:answer/id #uuid "9cde80f5-0a17-51cc-8fcb-e28702d3897c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500785} {:answer/id #uuid "e33da50a-6797-5e61-8dad-558084c1c0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e33da50a-6797-5e61-8dad-558084c1c0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500950} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f20aec0-879d-5670-bfea-c9c0def60bdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b5c204f-7839-5ec6-95c0-a9151439158a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97637" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(1,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(1,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,1i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=1.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=1$ and $\\omega =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123f9b2b-0563-52eb-a8a6-c11e35140af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500507} {:answer/id #uuid "31c4c2e2-f9c7-55fa-9b0b-965e7a60983f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500570} {:answer/id #uuid "6a44857f-c227-5ee8-907e-759c13f62a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500686} {:answer/id #uuid "d4901436-e9f5-5dcb-ad95-a9bd329e5817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500920} {:answer/id #uuid "ee63a80b-6b4b-5e42-89f8-7c9624056a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a44857f-c227-5ee8-907e-759c13f62a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500686} :answer-field/id #uuid "360e3f58-2f52-52b9-85ca-0ee548100f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6798c7d5-6dc4-50ce-934a-27d8986f8abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-116498" :question/problem "![](images/32/324e3b2752fd398e47ce066dfc44f8a4d96cc3d7916f917fa142485ae86bf48f.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the height is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is the surface area increasing when the dimensions of the rectangular prism are $2\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $7\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =7\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x,$ $y$ and $z.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2xz+2xy+2yz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(2x+2z)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(2z+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4)(-5)+(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 7)(11)+(2\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 4)(-5) \\\\  & =-60+198-110 \\\\  & =28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the solid is increasing at a rate of $28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eec3fb3-98bb-539f-828a-96203367f774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln(3)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eec3fb3-98bb-539f-828a-96203367f774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln(3)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500764} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bfa90a3-ba24-559f-9c8b-5a07e671e7fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501023} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cf7146-ed56-5694-a708-a9b3016a6fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11cf7146-ed56-5694-a708-a9b3016a6fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500506} :answer-field/id #uuid "504d5d52-6b4f-57e5-9c75-2dca13703f87" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc780555-5bb0-55aa-b97f-a47fa4610bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc780555-5bb0-55aa-b97f-a47fa4610bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500860} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4dc9af4-ea47-5fb1-8da7-ddafaed28423" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87416abb-dad9-5afe-bb9d-e8fcd5a9095c" :question/math-academy-id "q-350227" :question/problem "Given that  $f(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t)\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{6}{t}\\,k,$   what is ${f}^{′}(1)?$\n\n${f}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$j\\,\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$k$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t))\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{6}{t})\\,k \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t)+2)\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(1),$ we substitute $t=1$ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 1)+2)\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\,j-\\frac{6}{{1}^{2}}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡1\\,j-6\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f044fe5-a78a-503c-8136-573bff49033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500531} {:answer/id #uuid "2903205c-6d62-59f7-9321-c5aced82f76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500545} {:answer/id #uuid "31598524-9782-5a60-9550-6527d0e8e332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500569} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd5b608-b1ff-5a9d-9abd-12c753eba961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500731} {:answer/id #uuid "d37f3eb9-f956-5d80-bd59-824978a5c588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31598524-9782-5a60-9550-6527d0e8e332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500569} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60c8b13-b572-54f8-b997-4b367f683cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a02e865a-6a6d-5b10-8171-4b544dbd46b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-2795" :question/problem "![](images/52/52448daf1f9aea06b4ab4713756080ddb3ab465b4fae8f92b65a54c251a81d5e.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}$ and its height increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $50\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $15\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $5\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ y & =5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ z & =15\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-50{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & =(10)(5)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(10)(15)(1)+(15)(5)(-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $8\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f0ad0a-171c-5322-8cf2-cfca08972fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500510} {:answer/id #uuid "407711da-42ac-56dc-a3f6-af974631b602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500594} {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} {:answer/id #uuid "86e21ca8-366b-5985-a073-18a5cd00683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500749} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4c7ab4-1278-5730-804a-3c97ca86d365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcae1503-ee5b-5bcc-859b-64edce5a1f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3fef551-e9bd-56f6-a5fb-6131de39b9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86542" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨2,4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61117aac-0ddd-5b18-90b2-d250afca608b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500666} {:answer/id #uuid "70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500699} {:answer/id #uuid "c817eb95-9eb3-52b3-8f8b-7dc081fb2ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500891} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc3c7fc-fe9f-5c7b-8cbd-d47330b88535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500941} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ff4ab0-084f-5bf6-8cc1-99171d95158a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500699} :answer-field/id #uuid "e801f5f0-f458-5f7b-91b1-d27a8bacf367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8090d53-1681-5d0f-a69a-1950471f0a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-66523" :question/problem "Tom's basketball has a hole in it, meaning its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of a ball. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of the ball **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{(\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3})}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{12\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85efc12e-6686-5c83-a584-51d24265e514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500745} {:answer/id #uuid "87c47d54-c33c-5791-b51c-9f273e7288ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500752} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc4dbf-a3c1-5c4a-bb30-637dbdcc4445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500795} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7d23ab-bc7a-5e58-9183-a23a3316f17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500909} {:answer/id #uuid "ff089ad5-5112-577e-a4ad-ebbdd0861706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-{(x-6)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc4dbf-a3c1-5c4a-bb30-637dbdcc4445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500795} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e91312-58c2-59dc-9a23-0a2685890965" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aef8db66-e528-55a5-877b-5de5c3121fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140270" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-3x+1)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-3x+1) & =-({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb50cfa7-d5fd-5e1c-bd95-f2d2d5a2cd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb50cfa7-d5fd-5e1c-bd95-f2d2d5a2cd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500970} :answer-field/id #uuid "4367237c-774c-57e1-b55c-8026f7a5b4ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b06ab3d9-3b8c-5c9f-a694-75f899defb02" :question/math-academy-id "q-314513" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b45121fc7e3b6de3e37053b944bed610e55d337b7c73d89b258a89f903ad0c2.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $1.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}$ and its height is increasing at a rate of $1\\text{ feet/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $9{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min},$ how fast is its width decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{feet}$ in length, $3\\,\\text{feet}$ in width, and $2\\,\\text{feet}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of width is positive.\n\nThe width is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{feet}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ y & =3\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ z & =2\\,\\text{feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=3\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=2\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & =(5)(3)(1)+(5)(2)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(2)(3)(-1.5) \\\\ -9 & =15+10\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})-9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=2\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8dcca78-6127-52c8-a7fd-0263cd403f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8dcca78-6127-52c8-a7fd-0263cd403f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500963} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d83943b-fa05-5a32-815b-4e9cc7e9a241" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501041} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2625b9b2-ccd2-5138-938a-0f951e89b71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2625b9b2-ccd2-5138-938a-0f951e89b71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500543} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa7cf4a-4ced-579f-b3b1-a8420309518b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108be57e-a123-56a8-8b6d-ef26347eebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "108be57e-a123-56a8-8b6d-ef26347eebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500504} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a7e8649-8e07-5c84-b1f7-d22b5da0ebf7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "badd637f-464d-556b-9b5c-ae357a07836a" :question/math-academy-id "q-218760" :question/problem "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+8y-6$ by completing the square.\n\n$-3{y}^{2}+8y-6=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3$. So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}-\\,{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2) & =-3({(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59b992c6-a6c5-5a2a-bea8-00115a148a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500651} {:answer/id #uuid "89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500757} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ff4381-b2a5-5d9c-a6aa-03d02c2f7df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500797} {:answer/id #uuid "c4209c74-841c-5648-8653-0db964c34b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500878} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d8375a-8649-5db7-9cc9-00f70e9c364c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{6y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500757} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe16f66-cbec-5dab-913b-319990c8cc5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb3732e8-5921-5cbd-a1fe-55832a8ffa5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-144820" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin. The derivative at the point $(2,-4)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *slope of the line through $P(x,y)$ and the origin* is given by $\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot {(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(2,-4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=\\frac{k(-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d6b89b-9c2e-5338-b504-d4c40283ef6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "820{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500518} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2ce6b1-c4fc-5ed8-8a4c-3a4c5f749dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "525{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500526} {:answer/id #uuid "864515c1-3b8e-59c9-b866-916b8262d2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500747} {:answer/id #uuid "88a9531f-3fb7-5eed-9a5e-1179bfcc6d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,750{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500755} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a2b408-e5cd-52e5-aaa7-f0316c914927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "670{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864515c1-3b8e-59c9-b866-916b8262d2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500747} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d21bae5-4b6c-54e9-aab3-a599790bd158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd9599f1-ed67-5948-967c-4c445ad154d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-49012" :question/problem "![](images/06/06eb8269e36ecb0185c0bfa0069c36a705e36711ca0a705fba3e6eae7b6c946d.png)\n\nJohn is a carpenter who wants to modify a wooden box. The length, width and height of the box are decreased at a rate of $1\\text{ cm/s},$ $2\\text{ cm/s}$ and $4\\text{ cm/s},$ respectively. Determine the rate at which the volume of the box decreases, if its dimensions are $15\\text{ cm}$ long, $10\\text{ cm}$ wide and $15\\text{ cm}$ high." :question/worked-solution "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=15\\text{ cm},\\, & W=10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ H=15\\text{ cm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ cm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ cm/s},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(10)(15)(-1)+(15)(15)(-2)+(15)(10)(-4) \\\\  & =-1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the box is decreasing at a rate of $1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0863c307-3fc4-50bb-8193-2637b4d1fbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500482} {:answer/id #uuid "19bc5229-73fa-5621-ae34-90fcabb4ab10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500522} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44c7a4-9e6e-5b6f-85e7-5e7350d7fb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500565} {:answer/id #uuid "c0622a2f-34d6-58c4-b16a-d4b9c8c6f07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500866} {:answer/id #uuid "efca88fd-4fef-5d52-a234-b3f67f03da6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19bc5229-73fa-5621-ae34-90fcabb4ab10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500522} :answer-field/id #uuid "72fd739d-61ce-572d-ba84-147f6daa016b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e44f5e7f-50c8-5f61-b0b8-699aaec6f842" :question/math-academy-id "q-52504" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}}\\,$ given that $y<0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{y}\\cdot \\frac{3}{y}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{y})}^{2}} \\\\ &= |\\frac{3}{y}| \\\\ &= \\frac{|3|}{|y|} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y<0.$ To take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign, so we have $|y|=-y.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{|y|}=-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9e53027-3a83-5961-948b-e0b5df7093f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e53027-3a83-5961-948b-e0b5df7093f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501000} :answer-field/id #uuid "b852a09c-ea6b-5010-b9b3-a3b637cbd328" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4e15d3e-925b-56d4-bf57-253dd32acaf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343194" :question/problem "![](images/99/996768e4b1ce8ae57bcf3cce61059f8349c307ea9f05c9c1f51831ff6fd8ae68.png)\n\nA rectangular loaf of rising bread dough is squeezed between two rigid side panels that keep its width fixed. The length of the loaf is decreasing by $20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},$ while the $60\\,\\text{mm}$ width does not change. If the volume of the loaf is constant, how fast is its height changing when its dimensions are $100\\,\\text{mm}$ in length and $30\\,\\text{mm}$ in height?\n\nThe height is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =100\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ y & =60\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ z & =30\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =0\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is not changing and $y=60,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=60xz.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60xz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =60x(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+60z(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =60(100)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+60(30)(-20) \\\\ 0 & =6000(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})-36\\,000 \\\\ 36\\,000 & =6000(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the solid is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c949071-0d3c-54bb-9dc4-96b0d8539778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500621} {:answer/id #uuid "610bdf54-043a-5740-ad3a-0dec8d2b57b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500665} {:answer/id #uuid "663bf8f7-c948-5768-ae58-7d976d853146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500680} {:answer/id #uuid "cee0c053-9570-5242-9c89-c00687d42bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500911} {:answer/id #uuid "dde36f82-c9e7-56b6-95a2-ab881de27e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dde36f82-c9e7-56b6-95a2-ab881de27e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500939} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9e74ba-160b-54c7-b340-b16d836e9f06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ea3ea5b4-3a71-55fd-af96-eb417a578734" :question/math-academy-id "q-160895" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(0,0),$ $(2,-2),$ $(3,-1),$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y){e}^{x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(0,0) & =(0-0,0+0) \\\\  & =(0,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-2) & =(2-(-2),2+(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-1) & =(3-(-1),3+(-1)) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,1) & =(1-1,1+1) \\\\  & =(0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0b/0b23c6463447aa1fb64bdeeca689a40711f8dad59b348bb7943c2b9d2de7e8fc.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}u{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =4({e}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00561303-4d8b-5648-aef1-ded86fb68a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500470} {:answer/id #uuid "15ab2670-044a-519f-a892-d7c304eec4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500515} {:answer/id #uuid "c588653b-6c79-5422-bfe3-23e763ffffb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}+{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500885} {:answer/id #uuid "e7716d6f-5fc6-5f2a-ab7a-5ec082b205e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500959} {:answer/id #uuid "f65885a6-a87b-5af6-b23f-932e21b5918e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{93}{4}-{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7716d6f-5fc6-5f2a-ab7a-5ec082b205e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500959} :answer-field/id #uuid "d040eed3-515d-5c40-bf7b-ad0d3ff29d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaa23e3b-bd80-5fbc-b343-c6e4d7d3776d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140420" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}-7x-11$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+7x+11)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+11\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+11\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $11$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x-11 & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "442e3b6f-0334-59dc-bbe2-9c521ab09118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "442e3b6f-0334-59dc-bbe2-9c521ab09118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500602} :answer-field/id #uuid "41fb01ad-fc9d-5c1d-96c5-0e78cb67f17e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3d27d1e-a375-5722-8f78-27b79ebf66c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343180" :question/problem "![](images/49/4946691a4258fb310ec7052f1edd5a1b9117ca7daeea152e4a1b01b11a624e2e.png)\n\nJennie fills an aquarium, in the shape of a rectangular prism that is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $25\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, with water from a tap. What is the rate of change of the water level if the volume is increasing at a rate of $250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}?$\n\nThe water level is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =50\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =25\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=50\\,\\text{cm}$ and $y=25\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=(50)(25)z=1250z.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1250z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1250(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}250 & =1250(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =0.2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is increasing at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "260cd36f-3cd1-5e6e-a08e-7d3ca9370c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500542} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba08eda-a7a0-539e-92cd-414b03d79077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500722} {:answer/id #uuid "aea1e521-d9dc-5058-a940-b2513a2a3010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500832} {:answer/id #uuid "b17792ba-6da9-5712-933a-ecb6ba3164b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500837} {:answer/id #uuid "cb62be8e-3070-5be6-b38d-3a062278e1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb62be8e-3070-5be6-b38d-3a062278e1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500897} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6e4a4bf-3e09-5621-be81-4a8b258251af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc2b2629-e323-5ab5-9be3-1b12132dec56" :question/math-academy-id "q-140379" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}-4x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x) & =-8({(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121dd7b4-2544-54e9-b82c-91427f263603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501496} {:answer/id #uuid "17ac5bc2-5b2c-5952-9696-90205b58421a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501512} {:answer/id #uuid "69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501661} {:answer/id #uuid "711d3051-555f-5251-b3e7-5ba581b6dcac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501674} {:answer/id #uuid "dba0a80a-5b15-55dd-8f84-6cb26b9b34c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501661} :answer-field/id #uuid "60260284-1b87-5a9c-95ee-efdd32c08bc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0166ce2f-2829-5697-886e-176606ebec4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337311" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{1}{C}) \\\\ 2xy+{x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60afaad7-5c74-5ffe-abde-22de9074b994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501645} {:answer/id #uuid "727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501675} {:answer/id #uuid "87cd631b-b970-5565-be2b-b669ed3ae06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501727} {:answer/id #uuid "cc782d2e-ff11-5ac5-a0c7-6ccf48af546c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501874} {:answer/id #uuid "e50bcdd8-481a-5a75-a0f4-6d4a10f0bb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501675} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eaf0f19-6003-5585-a879-f271b5fc12a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b0e00ac-15e0-50ff-896e-2fb3f25eb38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336519" :question/problem "An ink reservoir for an industrial printer can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of ink solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved ink dye. A more concentrated ink solution with concentration $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+9t-6t=3(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =12(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =12(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 4(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 80+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{20}^{2}} & =-62 \\\\ C & =-62(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(20+t)-\\frac{62(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(20+t)-62{(\\frac{20}{20+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 60+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 60+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =90 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(20+30)-62{(\\frac{20}{20+30})}^{2} \\\\  & =200-62{(\\frac{20}{50})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈190.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa46099-da71-5b6e-bf88-ae9c1cd9cdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501493} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8aabfa-3b2a-546d-a525-0b6c5247d515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501570} {:answer/id #uuid "3c64925c-c3fb-50bc-a4cf-26896798c5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501573} {:answer/id #uuid "79afc2f1-79a1-5645-98b6-d23030be32d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501693} {:answer/id #uuid "e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501930} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f525641-4d00-5cf1-b519-6a6c8d3e8e86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11c8e0ff-5f12-56c5-bbf7-d578f71b8d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336746" :question/problem "A rinse tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+16t-24t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (30-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(30-t)^{-4}Q & =0.4(30-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3}) & =0.4(30-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(30-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-3} & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(30-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+C(30-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ C(30-0)^{3}+0.2(30-0) & =9 \\\\ C(30)^{3}+6 & =9 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}(30-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 30\\,(\\frac{30-t}{30})+3{(\\frac{30-t}{30})}^{3} \\\\  & =6\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+3{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =6,\\beta =3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =6-3=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a73b112-23d9-5ce7-b7f6-9cc9b1ea6ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501541} {:answer/id #uuid "5173cabb-209f-59b0-bb30-f3195ff21e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501614} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8cacfb-384f-5813-911b-4115a5b7ba6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501667} {:answer/id #uuid "8d016a81-6b30-515f-b97d-3efba5f2f640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501737} {:answer/id #uuid "f91702ba-eae7-53c8-b6a7-9bd48502b51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b8cacfb-384f-5813-911b-4115a5b7ba6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501667} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf72de5c-8edd-59cf-9c2b-dc6c69c9659d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22282018-2ca4-523a-8e4c-90a2c94b5770" :question/math-academy-id "q-68493" :question/problem "${(\\frac{2}{3})}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9}=\\frac{{2}^{7}}{{3}^{7}}\\cdot {3}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{2}^{7}}{{3}^{7}}\\cdot {3}^{9}={2}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{9}}{{3}^{7}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{2}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{9}}{{3}^{7}}={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9-7}={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01243280-a8f6-5503-94bb-a930a8a2f0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501466} {:answer/id #uuid "24e069a3-4467-5da5-aa50-4d23695fc6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501533} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9be344-8141-5390-b7cb-280192fa2e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501640} {:answer/id #uuid "850f976f-2f35-5bd0-8500-d963993c3749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501721} {:answer/id #uuid "c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501854} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c26a07-893b-5eda-88b9-2f21c73d94f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33804b3c-aea9-58d0-9654-8a84f729c14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337573" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3854e6c47f28ce601ef2395379bea788e954c4e4057331772b5840cd7073237.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05436460-8fd0-5eaf-8d02-d2f20514c78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501478} {:answer/id #uuid "134d188d-b9c3-552f-b921-1183370fb0c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501501} {:answer/id #uuid "21a0e148-8730-5869-a663-8249c3f4a7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501523} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b9e3db-75fc-500b-be46-121b7aace8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501799} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaec04f-5284-5518-89e1-55d6d0d563f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b9e3db-75fc-500b-be46-121b7aace8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501799} :answer-field/id #uuid "14daa8e3-e1bb-5707-81d3-5bbf9b88123d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "361640a2-f197-5749-a9e6-5d3176000185" :question/math-academy-id "q-357243" :question/problem "The temperature inside a freezer decreases by $\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times (-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 4+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{3}{5})=-\\frac{5\\times 3}{4\\times 5}=-\\frac{15}{20}=-\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4893c99a-7136-5bd1-aef4-b6e90b029371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501600} {:answer/id #uuid "531fe1e5-e3d5-5649-b313-13cd6d8f7f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501619} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501669} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0fae8f-9d6c-5176-a980-fcb53f0d4c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501850} {:answer/id #uuid "dde6aaf3-b2a8-57d1-a021-79d4beafe63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501669} :answer-field/id #uuid "f427581a-7c85-5678-8de9-9e496c491b50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3933c79b-01f9-56d3-9429-e1ebc3d9a1ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-337317" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C),$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C)) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x>0,$ $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\ne 0,$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0064f3e5-7e46-560e-af4f-5f1bc6505542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.10\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501463} {:answer/id #uuid "457c4add-95f2-5630-82a0-e8ce4e4d81a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.91\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501592} {:answer/id #uuid "6005b2ab-465f-5cd8-bb5d-d54c1eeed27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.35\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501642} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ea94bf-a047-5c90-b7d0-f7b804cd2312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.48\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501829} {:answer/id #uuid "d94e5053-03cf-5ba6-ada7-c621dbe03ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.72\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "457c4add-95f2-5630-82a0-e8ce4e4d81a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.91\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501592} :answer-field/id #uuid "752081da-3806-5149-83db-ac2d9b472911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502017}] :question/id #uuid "4779b0e3-7c68-54eb-98ad-ac554dad498b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336685" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt.\nFresh water is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that fresh water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; water enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+12t-12t=420\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{1}{35}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{35} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{35})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{35}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{35}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{t}{35}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/35},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=14,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =14 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/35} & =14 \\\\ A & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=14{e}^{-t/35}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(20)=14{e}^{-20/35}≈7.91\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502119 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2268a62f-af3b-5554-9392-93550ebd3f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501524} {:answer/id #uuid "34f958da-773f-515f-abd1-d2b6edb53bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501563} {:answer/id #uuid "8075dcdc-4e30-5eab-9850-ab4d6e913258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501711} {:answer/id #uuid "a46483a5-c5e1-536a-a0d5-b280e918e22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501784} {:answer/id #uuid "ab74afde-81f9-5e89-9f65-02cca1c6532c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a46483a5-c5e1-536a-a0d5-b280e918e22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501784} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae216ed6-c5e7-5d88-9e19-dcdfe886b809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b837a54-b40c-5dbd-9c93-92d2c8b04271" :question/math-academy-id "q-357241" :question/problem "The temperature of a chemical solution decreases by $\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 4+3}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5})=-\\frac{15\\times 4}{4\\times 5}=-\\frac{60}{20}=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-3{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd464ab-03dd-5c29-a547-e17631549a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.20\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501521} {:answer/id #uuid "41e819ff-cd32-5d60-ba16-54665c890b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.84\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501588} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba3054a-6459-569b-81c3-907904cb6174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.78\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501735} {:answer/id #uuid "91dfca2e-8991-5e9e-966d-e682738680c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.12\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501745} {:answer/id #uuid "92fa06dd-98f7-529e-8d06-b10b01e3a805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.05\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fd464ab-03dd-5c29-a547-e17631549a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.20\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501521} :answer-field/id #uuid "396eb760-1ba4-5c61-a4fd-b36963318cca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501992}] :question/id #uuid "9f051584-9c22-538b-be73-a90ad4399f9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336843" :question/problem "A $300\\,\\text{gal}$-capacity tank initially holds $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water containing $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water; clean water enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed water leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=(180+2t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{90+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{90+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(90+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=45,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =45 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(90+0)^{2}} & =45 \\\\ A & =45(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{45(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 180+2{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =45{(\\frac{90}{90+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{90}{150})}^{2} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =16.20\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0112f1fc-6de5-513e-87f9-3b66ed92fd30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501465} {:answer/id #uuid "26fdd352-98f8-5049-9bca-4712271dc3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501536} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc4d99a-a70c-5a49-aae0-f3604e67d27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501638} {:answer/id #uuid "c69e4261-0557-5f97-9e81-25b3bb4c21af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501861} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501952} :answer-field/id #uuid "507a90fb-d7f1-59f5-8df1-dc619644c3da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3e80580-6c31-591c-a6d6-df75a9aede3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336503" :question/problem "A fertilizer tank for an irrigation system can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of fertilizer solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved fertilizer. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=90+18t-12t=6(15+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(15+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|15+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(15+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(15+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q) & =(15+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (15+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(15+t)Q & =9(15+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2}) & =9(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(15+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(15+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(15+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(15+t)+\\frac{C}{(15+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of fertilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 3(15+0)+\\frac{C}{(15+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 45+\\frac{C}{(15)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{15}^{2}} & =-27 \\\\ C & =-27(15)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(15+t)-\\frac{27(15)^{2}}{(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(15+t)-27{(\\frac{15}{15+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 90+18{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 90+6{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3(15+20)-27{(\\frac{15}{15+20})}^{2} \\\\  & =105-27{(\\frac{15}{35})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈100.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04836823-2fb1-5c3d-a0c5-77a932219b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501476} {:answer/id #uuid "0761bbe1-fc86-571b-915d-d390a147033c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501484} {:answer/id #uuid "6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501643} {:answer/id #uuid "a8371a77-d019-545f-94c5-55f27b9ed753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501795} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0edf3-56a1-540a-ba78-687280e03d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501643} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2e9024-0792-5fc6-9ce4-361c190fbfa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc13bee1-bf2a-51a5-a80d-77b695b1c60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337315" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C,$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(C) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8297df33-3945-557a-8b12-47cba0ef0ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501715} {:answer/id #uuid "cf62465d-ee5a-56f9-9205-cdbc67e9ed15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501877} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b02612-0eeb-5065-9a7a-a11e62b8557c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501924} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c706d3-0dd0-5ebe-87cc-56a68f59d32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501938} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c9430f-5d3d-51ad-9d23-0d3aa66de7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c9430f-5d3d-51ad-9d23-0d3aa66de7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501948} :answer-field/id #uuid "2536844b-ad4a-5ed6-bce8-3971a2720b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d71152b6-16b1-5e4e-a992-55dfd11f6205" :question/math-academy-id "q-357085" :question/problem "The temperature of a liquid decreases by $\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $\\frac{8}{3}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $\\frac{8}{3}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{4}).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{4})=-\\frac{8\\times 3}{3\\times 4}=-\\frac{8}{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035b5582-aa17-529b-b101-df7971bd9920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{76}{21}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501471} {:answer/id #uuid "0792a188-0269-58cf-97bd-516b8fe89cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501485} {:answer/id #uuid "2881adf2-dc78-54da-838f-2275d55fed05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501539} {:answer/id #uuid "57cf6cec-0ed9-5d2e-af3f-5ae7420f7f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{76}{21}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501630} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9bde92-27d8-57f7-b7da-25ad1a8a9ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{20}{7}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0792a188-0269-58cf-97bd-516b8fe89cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501485} :answer-field/id #uuid "7180b989-67d5-5e43-9459-6d581302e067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8734cff-4115-50f2-9d56-00580f475192" :question/math-academy-id "q-357194" :question/problem "A hot air balloon descends $\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ each second. What is the total change in altitude after $\\frac{9}{7}$ seconds?" :question/worked-solution "The hot air balloon descends $\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ each second.\n\nSince the altitude decreases, the change each second is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nThe descent lasts $\\frac{9}{7}$ seconds, so the total change in altitude is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{7}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3}).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{7}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3})=-\\frac{9\\times 7}{7\\times 3}=-\\frac{9}{3}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in altitude is $-3\\,\\text{m}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78a9c22a-d2cf-5556-83bc-973ce5081e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98.74\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501692} {:answer/id #uuid "af336e1d-577f-5f06-a6fb-d40be71dd596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.90\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501812} {:answer/id #uuid "db9870bb-ca97-5434-9e2f-f0f1821c5287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.12\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501907} {:answer/id #uuid "dc627a42-82c5-5373-81cf-850a6f244c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.83\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501909} {:answer/id #uuid "f405b4b6-a9a5-5b98-9592-9a6a03980d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.65\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78a9c22a-d2cf-5556-83bc-973ce5081e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98.74\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501692} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc2b09f-c2d7-5d6c-8b4d-48c2f11ae6c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501998}] :question/id #uuid "ea65973e-51d0-5969-ad2d-3fe183a87ba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336840" :question/problem "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity water tank initially holds $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water containing $210\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,200+90t-60t=30(240+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{240+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{240+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{240+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|240+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(240+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $210\\,\\text{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=210,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =210 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(240+0)^{2}} & =210 \\\\ A & =210(240)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{210(240)^{2}}{(240+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(240+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 240+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =110\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(110) & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+110})}^{2} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{24}{35})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈98.74\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5310661c-c40f-57d8-961e-c3c723529252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501618} {:answer/id #uuid "80e52af3-e585-5a0d-aa22-4ea4ca9a03f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501713} {:answer/id #uuid "8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501738} {:answer/id #uuid "e51a135b-6f9a-5f82-ace0-3594f1b83adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501929} {:answer/id #uuid "f1216dab-2435-53da-876f-2a411b9c344f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501738} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6a4d2e-c7d0-561a-ba37-0ecf2d6f5d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f66e36cd-9bf6-56e0-b87a-016d297333d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337313" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a455916-32b4-5125-a03d-acadaf8f7915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502359} {:answer/id #uuid "74dbaa7e-fca7-5073-a877-6b16ff3e4d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502367} {:answer/id #uuid "8a06c96f-4a15-5bf2-a6d0-edbdb248951e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502372} {:answer/id #uuid "90fb3517-b11c-5317-ac7d-17e978e3d179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502376} {:answer/id #uuid "c45caa27-da31-57ac-8666-1a0bb5c8d193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c45caa27-da31-57ac-8666-1a0bb5c8d193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502404} :answer-field/id #uuid "139bd750-f127-56f4-9c8c-dad3ecb6aa91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "15405bfd-218a-5ebb-ac44-be3eba615aca" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d62b7f63213e0a36a4cc186c396de6986f8c9e594fa045791d9c37fad7550f60.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/ae/ae31ae08afde8173954eef7609108f51e2a5113e7ef37a815511d29e653f33bf.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=0.5$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{2})^2+(\\frac{3}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44542f74-71e7-507c-ba2a-4a28f636ac89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502352} {:answer/id #uuid "57d39e45-b7dc-5b05-accd-6ded543fb154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502357} {:answer/id #uuid "c55ad23b-68a1-557a-aa55-3f07c42db8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502405} {:answer/id #uuid "e16574a4-34e8-54cf-a77e-3ad1ef0fe437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502420} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b27dcd-fc04-5048-9e40-7aa9d5043980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e16574a4-34e8-54cf-a77e-3ad1ef0fe437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502420} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d9330f-8252-5b46-9c3b-ad958ccbb399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25059bd6-40b8-5009-96b3-70ae023f7607" :question/problem "![](images/35/35ce1ab5c1689dca152c58e955e4a43c0be0e2bc6a796bf20e84f2c99786f8ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/9b/9b906579f40ce2a1c59129d80540b339c1bb00fcd9a5f5ce179b01efeb702ab5.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=3.5$ and $BH=0.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{7}{2})^2+(\\frac{1}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aeab7be-8c7d-5a3a-be9f-120546db36cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{17}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502337} {:answer/id #uuid "78dffdfd-3b80-57ec-a318-d95ddef548d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502369} {:answer/id #uuid "b21b2119-51aa-5753-ab74-53f404fe7b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502390} {:answer/id #uuid "c0949ae1-36a1-539c-9ddd-694612a88e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502402} {:answer/id #uuid "dc94450a-5a6d-5d38-bd5d-47e15a6a879e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{17}}{17}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78dffdfd-3b80-57ec-a318-d95ddef548d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502369} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf6beda-d9da-55d2-92ef-7c06f8adde74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e59a005-0b33-5ed7-ad5d-fc08843b1882" :question/problem "![](images/11/1112fef974be645940a11f8f3f099ca6b0125a6a92ca8642712dde7b86c63ac8.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/4c/4c8930d8535e42d793def554d8e65943e637c02debb6d411048096ba1b8cda68.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=2$ and $BH=0.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2)^2+(\\frac{1}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36d788b6-d7d5-53f9-baa9-94394f07c1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502346} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac393dd-d1cc-54b8-bd96-c686fc1523d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{109}}{109}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502348} {:answer/id #uuid "5612fc05-2875-50ee-bed6-ce8a32dc5501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{109}}{2}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502355} {:answer/id #uuid "b70bf890-655a-5da2-bb16-078c7a660b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{109}}{109}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502395} {:answer/id #uuid "e29a47d6-0232-5e4f-87d6-8ea17544ebfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{109}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ac393dd-d1cc-54b8-bd96-c686fc1523d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{109}}{109}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502348} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5db592a-0691-5283-a627-c1c5c2ea250f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "570883a2-63cb-5ec8-886b-b501a7b97685" :question/problem "![](images/36/364b9ef322e1d6c1aaa54e23a2c09bf086f3721370da7b69aa7036222d1e4318.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/89/894f70223b9b29220ee424f53c1ae82ff804fc9b7ca3638e43481d7cea63f66a.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=5$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(5)^2+(\\frac{3}{2})^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{109}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{109}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{109}}{109}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10044cc3-3b39-5d32-a498-68916d941200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{13}}{13}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502329} {:answer/id #uuid "17c2381